---
scope: src/cli/init
status: aligned
description: "Cold-start npx entry point that drives the install service end-to-end from a plain terminal, before any agent session exists."
intent: >
  cli/init is the install consumer for users at a fresh terminal who
  have not yet started an agent session. It accepts a brain-integration
  selector, drives the shared install service to completion, applies
  every plan the service returns, and surfaces the follow-up surfaces
  the consumer must run later for any category the cold context cannot
  finish. Because no agent is present to interview the user, cli/init
  never prompts for backend-specific values; whatever the cold pass
  cannot resolve is deferred, named, and routed onward.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - A single npx invocation takes a cold project to AIDE-ready end-to-end with no Claude Code session involved. The brain-integration selector is the only knob the consumer accepts; running npx is itself the opt-in.
    - Every category whose plan the cold context can apply is applied; categories that cannot finish without a live agent or backend-specific input are deferred without prompting. The consumer never invents answers the install service has not authorized.
    - On exit the consumer surfaces, per deferred or skipped category, the named follow-up surface the user must invoke next. The user finishes a cold install knowing exactly what command picks up where the terminal stopped.
  undesired:
    - The consumer prompts for backend-specific values (paths, tokens, connection URIs) at install time. Cold-terminal context has no agent to interview the user; any such prompt is a category that should have deferred.
    - A category the install service marked deferred is silently dropped instead of surfaced as a follow-up. The user finishes the install believing it completed and discovers later that an unfinished step was never named.
    - The consumer mutates host files the install service did not return as a plan step — sneaking writes in to "save the user a command" rather than honoring the service's plan-as-contract.
---
